, "incumbency": -4.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": -0.36, "sigma_race": 5.7, "sigma_total": 6.09, "baseline_margin": -0.48, "analyst_adjustment": 0.48, "key_factors": ["Lombardo's incumbency advantage offsets a Democratic-leaning midterm environment", "Recent nonpartisan polls show a dead heat, with margins within two points", "Prediction markets lean slightly to Ford while six of seven raters call it a tossup", "October developments — Obama's endorsement, clemency and AI data-center controversies — keep the race fluid"], "rationale": "Everything points to a dead heat: September surveys from nonpartisan pollsters sit within two points either way, six of seven professional raters call it a tossup, and Lombardo's incumbency edge roughly cancels a Democratic-friendly environment. Two of three prediction markets tilt marginally to Ford, which offsets the slight Republican edge in polling averages and lands the expected margin at even.", "watch": "Post-September polling, or movement on the economy, the clemency story, or the AI data-center debate, could break the tie in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Joe Lombardo (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nevada's governor's race is one of the closest in the country. Incumbent Republican Joe Lombardo is seeking a second term against Democrat Aaron Ford, with independent Danielle Ford also on the ballot. The state's near-even partisan lean and a Democratic-favorable midterm environment largely cancel out, while Lombardo's incumbency adds a Republican edge. Six of seven professional raters call the race a tossup, with one rating it a tilt toward Republicans.\n\nRecent nonpartisan polls from September show margins within two points either way, and published averages put Lombardo ahead by about a point or less. Prediction markets are split but lean slightly toward Ford, and October developments — Barack Obama's endorsement of Ford, favorable editorial endorsements, and controversies over clemency a